    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d389">

                <pb facs="0676" n="548" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg548"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 389.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to
                        Mr. Bassett</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-01-21"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 21,
                        1885</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    letter of the 13th instant, in relation to the case of Mr. C. A. Van Bokkelen,
                    imprisoned at Port-au-Prince for debt.</p>
                <p>Although I have given your letter an attentive perusal, I find myself unable to
                    transfer hither the discussion of the merits of Mr. Van Bokkelen’s case, as your
                    comments would seem to indicate. I content myself by saying, therefore, that Mr.
                    Langston’s actions and protests in behalf of Mr. Van Bokkelen have received the
                    Department’s approval.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
